{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "resolution_hours",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.95,
      "source_groundedness": 0.55,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "A continuous resolution-time measure is the central axis for an efficiency query, giving strong grouping/modeling leverage. However, the values are not grounded: in every evidence sample opened_at is not shown, and the only timestamps (sys_updated_on and closed_at) are identical, so a specific figure like 161.13 or 384.32 hours cannot be derived from the visible source text. Likely computed from opened_at (plausible original column) but the samples don't confirm it, and the large spread of unique values suggests possible fabrication."
    },
    {
      "name": "resolution_speed",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
      "source_groundedness": 0.6,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "A Fast/Moderate/Slow bucketing of resolution time is a useful categorical axis for comparing efficiency across groups. Groundedness inherits from resolution_hours; bucketing itself is a warranted transformation, but rests on the same unverified duration. The Moderate label at 58h vs Slow at 145h is internally consistent."
    },
    {
      "name": "priority_level",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.3,
      "source_groundedness": 0.75,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Priority already exists as an original column, making this a near-redundant restatement with limited new leverage. Groundedness is mostly fine as a copy/parse of priority, but the first sample shows priority_level=1 for an incident where is_critical logic and other fields don't obviously establish P1, and no priority is shown in the sample source text, leaving some values unverifiable."
    },
    {
      "name": "is_critical",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.4,
      "source_groundedness": 0.45,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "A binary criticality flag could help segment high-impact incidents, but it is largely derivable from priority and is a coarse restatement. Groundedness is weak: 421 of 500 rows flagged critical, and a 'Printer not working properly' at priority 2 is marked is_critical=1, which contradicts a sensible reading of the source text and suggests a mislabeled/over-broad threshold."
    },
    {
      "name": "assignee_caseload",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
      "source_groundedness": 0.7,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Per-assignee case counts give a genuine new axis (workload vs resolution efficiency), highly relevant to the query. Derivable by aggregating assigned_to over the table, a warranted computation, though the specific counts can't be verified from single-row samples."
    },
    {
      "name": "category_volume",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.8,
      "source_groundedness": 0.75,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Category frequency is a reasonable new axis for spotting high-volume categories to prioritize for efficiency gains. Derivable by counting category across rows; the two printer rows sharing 406 is consistent with an aggregate count."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.72,
  "source_groundedness": 0.62,
  "rationale": "The set offers several genuinely useful axes for an efficiency analysis: a continuous resolution time, its speed bucket, assignee caseload, and category volume all provide real grouping/modeling leverage. priority_level and is_critical add little beyond the existing priority column and drag usefulness down. Groundedness is the bigger concern: resolution_hours produces specific multi-hour figures that cannot be reconstructed from the visible timestamps (which are identical) without an opened_at reference the samples don't show, and is_critical over-flags routine, low-priority incidents (e.g., a P2 printer issue) as critical, contradicting a sound reading of the text."
}
